  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$16.77B** of iron and steel in 2025, down 14.4% from 2024 and 20% below the 2022 peak of $20.89B. A third of it (**33%**, $5.45B) was ferrous scrap (7204), shipped in bulk to electric-arc-furnace mills in Türkiye, Thailand, India, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Taiwan and Viet Nam. Most of the rest was flat-rolled steel — hot-rolled, galvanized and alloy sheet — for Mexico and Canada, which together took **58%**. Exports have picked up in 2026: $12.72B from January to August, up 11.6%.\n\nScrap enters every large scrap market duty-free, so there the buyer's import licence, inspection and radiation rules decide access. Finished steel is where tariffs bite. **Canada** has surtaxed U.S. steel since March 2025, at 50% since September 8, 2026, even when it qualifies for CUSMA. **Mexico** still takes qualifying steel free, but its general rate rose to 35% in 2026, so the USMCA rule of origin matters more. The **EU** caps steel imports with quotas and a 50% duty above them, and **India** adds a safeguard duty on flat steel.",
